Body:
For responses api, you can use "@ai-sdk/openai" directly, IMO.

For example:
```nix
custom-provider = {
  npm = "@ai-sdk/openai";
  name = "OpenAI proxy";
    options = {
      apiKey = "{file:...}";
      baseURL = "https://.../v1";
    };
    models = {
      "gpt-5.2-codex" = {
         name = "GPT!";
        };
     };
 };
```

Guys, opencode scopes the commands to the working directory of the project. 

If you run opencode directly on your home directory, those things will keep happening.

@rekram1-node maybe we need to add some warning or disclaimer when launching opencode on the $HOME ?

this is my opencode.json

Operating System: ubuntu
Terminal: zsh

```
  "provider": {
    "deepseek": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "DeepSeek",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "https://api.deepseek.com/v1",
        "apiKey": "{env:DEEPSEEK_API_KEY}"
      },
      "models": {
        "deepseek-chat": {
          "name": "DeepSeek V3",
          "limit": {
            "context": 128000,
            "output": 8192
          }
        },
        "deepseek-reasoner": {
          "name": "DeepSeek R1",
          "limit": {
            "context": 128000,
            "output": 65536
          }
        }
      }
    },
```
